You have an expert understanding of plausible real-world social situations and outcomes. Your task is to read human-written descriptions of social-moral dilemmas and develop alternate versions of them while keeping the same basic scenario and context. The fact that it is a dilemma means that there is a primary action choice taken by an agent that will lead to some type of harm in either case, whether it differs in the patient of the harm (which could be the agent themself) or the stakes or temporality or some other quality of the harm, but a choice must be made. The objective is to have multiple versions of the same base ‘seed’ scenario that present a moral dilemma of some kind, where certain aspects of the scenario are parametrically varied such that the stakes and/or causal structure of the scenario changes along tightly controllable and qualitatively salient axes.

Take a look at an example input scenario:

“There was a time when I was working somewhere and another person in the same department was constantly out “sick” on a regular basis, at least once or twice a week. At the time, there were only the two of us in the department. Since the person was barely at work, their job responsibilities fell on my shoulders. It was a difficult situation because I also had my job responsibilities to complete as well as supervise students. I was frustrated and annoyed with the person because I believed they did not have a legitimate excuse. One day, the individual claimed they were not feeling well, but then later on in the afternoon, she called me to talk. The person continued to talk on the phone stating she was feeling better and wanted to know what was going on. At this point, I was so annoyed, I made an excuse and hung up the phone. I had a feeling that she was not really sick, but lazy and didn’t want to work. I didn’t know whether or not to tell my supervisor or just let her eventually get caught in her lies.”

First, you must first reframe the content according to the following base rules, if it doesn’t already follow them: 
1. The scenario must be in first person (i.e. the agent must be “I”)
2. The outcome of the action must be removed (i.e. the benefit or harm resulting from the action must be removed), and 
3. The action must be pulled out as a binary choice. The binary choice must not contain “I” and always be a simple verb sentence (e.g. “play the piano” or “not play the piano”; in essence, the action choice must make sense if we prepend the words “I decide to” to it). 

Then, the scenario must be formatted into the following json structure:

```
{
    "text": "reframed scenario content",
    "options": {
        "1": "binary action choice 1",
        "2": "binary action choice 2"
    }
}
```

